zle\n\nEach input contains two kinds of things:\n\n1. **Hollow rectangles (frames).** These are the large single-colour objects with an empty interior, such as the 2-frame and the 3-frame in pair 1.\n2. **Scattered single pixels** in several colours. In pair 1 these are the 8s and 4s.\n\nIn every output, all the scattered pixels are gone. Each frame's interior is filled with a checkerboard in one of the scattered colours. The checkerboard always has a filled cell in the interior's top-left corner.\n\n**Which colour goes into which frame?** I counted cells to find out.\n\n| Pair | Frame interior | Checkerboard cells | Scattered colour with that count |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 3×3 (frame 2) | 5 | 8 appears 5 times |\n| 1 | 2×7 (frame 3) | 7 | 4 appears 7 times |\n| 2 | 2×5 (frame 8) | 5 | 3 appears 5 times |\n| 2 | 6×5 (frame 4) | 15 | 6 appears 15 times |\n| 3 | 1×1 (frame 1) | 1 | 4 appears once |\n| 3 | 1×3 (frame 8) | 2 | 2 appears twice |\n\nSo the rule is: **a frame takes the scattered colour whose pixel count equals the number of cells in its interior checkerboard.** In effect the loose pixels are \"collected\" and laid out inside the matching frame. This also explains the fact sheet's transition counts, for example 4→0 ×7 and 0→4 ×7 in pair 1: exactly the pixels that disappear reappear inside the frame.\n\n## Applying it to the test\n\n- **Frame 1** has a 4×7 interior, which gives 14 checkerboard cells. Colour 4 appears 14 times, so it gets colour 4.\n- **Frame 3** has a 6×9 interior, which gives 27 cells. Colour 5 appears 27 times, so it gets colour 5.\n- **Frame 2** has a 2×2 interior, which gives 2 cells. Colour 8 appears twice, so it gets colour 8.\n\nEach count is unique, so the matching is unambiguous.\n\n## How the program builds the output\n\n1. It separates objects of size 1 (the scattered pixels) from larger objects (the frames). It counts the scattered pixels per colour.\n2. It erases all scattered pixels and emits that canvas.\n3. For each frame, it computes the interior checkerboard cells (where the offset parity from the interior's top-left is even). It picks the colour whose count matches, fills those cells, and emits the canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    singles = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 1]\n    frames = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] > 1]\n    counts = {}\n    for o in singles:\n        counts[o[\"colour\"]] = counts.get(o[\"colour\"], 0) + 1\n    out = g.copy()\n    for o in singles:\n        y, x = o[\"cells\"][0]\n        out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Remove all scattered single pixels, after counting them per colour.\")\n    for f in frames:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = f[\"box\"]\n        iy0, ix0, iy1, ix1 = y0 + 1, x0 + 1, y1 - 1, x1 - 1\n        cells = [(y, x) for y in range(iy0, iy1 + 1) for x in range(ix0, ix1 + 1)\n                 if (y - iy0 + x - ix0) % 2 == 0]\n        n = len(cells)\n        col = None\n        for c, k in counts.items():\n            if k == n:\n                col = c\n                break\n        if col is None:\n            continue\n        for y, x in cells:\n            out[y, x] = col\n        emit(out, f\"Frame of colour {f['colour']} has {n} checkerboard interior cells; fill them with colour {col}, which had {n} scattered pixels.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Hollow rectangles (frames).** These are the large single-colour objects with an empty interior, such as the 2-frame and the 3-frame in pair 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each frame's interior is filled with a checkerboard in one of the scattered colours."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The checkerboard always has a filled cell in the interior's top-left corner."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"| Pair | Frame interior | Checkerboard cells | Scattered colour with that count |"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: **a frame takes the scattered colour whose pixel count equals the number of cells in its interior checkerboard.** In effect the loose pixels are \"collected\" and laid out inside the matching frame."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"This also explains the fact sheet's transition counts, for example 4→0 ×7 and 0→4 ×7 in pair 1: exactly the pixels that disappear reappear inside the frame."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"1bf48e9093ae0ddb1a3769ae96649d0e68aff30b49d00e95eab22535ff8d369f","task_sha256":"2456ce1bf8a1d7cdc29a5921e1d1a0d1bf27bfc079fca886ce7f8df734474bd6","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
